#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/polymorphism-kinds.md:1
msgid "Kinds of Polymorphism"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/polymorphism-kinds.md:3
msgid "In Rust we have 3 different mechanisms for writing polymorphic code:"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/polymorphism-kinds.md:5
msgid ""
"**Generics** - Static polymorphism where code abstracts over types but the "
"types are fully known at compile time."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/polymorphism-kinds.md:7
msgid ""
"**Enums** - Dynamic polymorphism where a fixed set of known types are "
"selected between at runtime."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/polymorphism-kinds.md:9
msgid ""
"**`dyn`** - Dynamic polymorphism where any type meeting a particular trait "
"interface can be used."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/polymorphism-kinds.md:14
msgid ""
"When discussing polymorphism in Rust, it's helpful to differentiate between "
"**static** polymorphism and **dynamic** polymorphism."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/polymorphism-kinds.md:17
msgid ""
"**static polymorphism** is when we abstract over types, but the type "
"information is fully known by the compiler. This allows us to reuse code in "
"different type contexts without needing to add any runtime overhead, and we "
"have access to the full set of features that traits expose. This is "
"accomplished with **generics** in Rust."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/polymorphism-kinds.md:23
msgid ""
"**dynamic polymorphism** is when we need to select between different types "
"at runtime, and we don't know at compile time which specific type will be "
"used."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/polymorphism-kinds.md:26
msgid ""
"When we have a fixed set of known types to choose from, we can use **enums** "
"to track at runtime which one we have."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/polymorphism-kinds.md:28
msgid ""
"When we don't know ahead of time which types may be used, e.g. if downstream "
"users may introduce new types that we don't know about, then we use "
"**`dyn`** to allow extensibility."
msgstr ""
